<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erwin Rommel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1891">1891</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Johannes Erwin Eugen Rommel, The Desert Fox, Der Wüstenfuchs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Erwin Rommel was a German field marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, born in Heidenheim in the Kingdom of Württemberg, who became known as the Desert Fox. Awarded the Pour le Mérite on the Italian Front in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, he published Infantry Attacks in 1937, commanded the 7th Panzer Division in the 1940 invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and led German and Italian forces in the North African campaign. In June 1944 he commanded the German forces opposing the Allied invasion of Normandy. Implicated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/20-july-plot">20 July plot</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>, he was given a choice between suicide and a trial, and took a cyanide pill. Historians dispute the postwar picture of him as an apolitical commander, which some call the Rommel myth.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rommel was born on 15 November 1891 in Heidenheim, 45 kilometres from Ulm, the third of five children of Erwin Rommel senior and Helene von Luz. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in the Romanian campaign and on the Italian front, using heavy covering fire with rapid advances and moving quickly to a flanking position to reach the rear of enemy positions. He received the Iron Cross, Second Class, for actions in September 1914 and January 1915, and the Pour le Mérite on 18 December 1917.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1919 he restored order at Friedrichshafen among rebellious and pro-communist sailors, and he returned Lindau to the legitimate government by negotiating with its city council rather than storming it. He became a Generalmajor in August 1939 and commanded the battalion guarding Hitler's headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. He then lobbied for a panzer division and got one, although the army's personnel office had offered a mountain division. On 6 February 1941 he was appointed commander of the Afrika Korps, sent to support Italian forces after their defeats in Operation Compass, and his operations in the Western Desert earned him the nickname Desert Fox.</p><p>He later commanded Army Group B in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from August 1943, and in November 1943 he became General Inspector of the Western Defences with authority to inspect the Atlantic Wall. His conduct of war is debated. Many authors describe him as chivalrous, and Dennis Showalter notes that both sides in the desert at first tried to wage a comparatively clean war, a war without hate, as Rommel put it. Stories of a Rommel treasure are traced instead to SS units under Walter Rauff, and no evidence links Rommel to them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>How far Rommel was involved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/20-july-plot">20 July plot</a> is disputed, since most conspirators were killed and the documents are fragmentary. A Court of Military Honour that included Wilhelm Keitel and Gerd von Rundstedt considered his case, and Hitler judged that executing him as a traitor would damage morale and offered him the chance to take his own life. He died on 14 October 1944 and received a state funeral. The Field Marshal Rommel Barracks at Augustdorf, the German Army's largest base, was named for him in 1961.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Erwin Rommel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
